FP32 libm transcriptions -- Arm optimized-routines

Several schemes are graded bitwise against a gfortran/glibc reference,
so WOOF carries hand transcriptions of the standard single-precision
libm routines rather than calling CUDA's, whose expf, powf and tgammaf
are measurably different functions. The transcriptions live in
woof/core/noahmp_libm.py, woof/core/mynn_pbl.py, woof/core/ruc.py,
in woof/core/kernels/glibc_flt32.cuh, and -- duplicated per kernel,
because CUDA translation units do not share device code -- in the .cu
files under woof/core/kernels/.
tests/test_cuda_libm_table_copies.py enumerates the table copies and
holds them to one spelling, so the scope of this section is
machine-checkable rather than prose.

The logf, expf, exp2f and powf cores, and their data tables, are Arm's
work. They were written by Szabolcs Nagy and published at
https://github.com/ARM-software/optimized-routines (math/logf.c,
math/expf.c, math/exp2f.c, math/powf.c and the corresponding
math/*_data.c) in August 2017, then imported into glibc for 2.27/2.28
by their own author. WOOF transcribed them from glibc's copy at
sysdeps/ieee754/flt-32/, which reproduces Arm's text unchanged apart
from symbol renaming, branch-hint and cast macro spellings, and
symbol-versioning declarations -- none of which WOOF reproduces. Those
transcriptions appear in fifteen files under woof/core/kernels/ --
glibc_flt32.cuh,
mynn_dmp_sibling.cu, mynn_pbl.cu, noahmp_bareflux.cu,
noahmp_fluxprep.cu, noahmp_leaves.cu, noahmp_radiation.cu,
noahmp_snow.cu, noahmp_soilwater.cu, noahmp_vegeflux.cu,
noahmp_vegprecip.cu, noahmp_water.cu, rrtmg_lw.cu, rrtmg_sw.cu and
vert_interp.cu (the logf core of the GPU vertical interpolation) -- and
once more, in Python, in woof/core/noahmp_libm.py. Sixteen files, and the
count is machine-derived: those are exactly the files that reproduce Arm's
coefficient tables.

vert_interp.cu's wrf_vi_pressure_log uses the Arm logf tables from
glibc_flt32.cuh and reproduces its range reduction and polynomial with
fused binary64 operations.

The sinf / cosf cores and their tables in
woof/core/kernels/glibc_trig_flt32.cuh are also Arm's work. Their sources
are math/sinf.c, math/cosf.c, math/sincosf.h and math/sincosf_data.c.
The 2018 Arm revision 5e8389113b47a48622d1de8beb9a4adc2b332694
predates glibc's imports ea5c662c623125ad39fc43d5b9224150256407a9
(shared data and helpers, 2018-08-10) and
599cf3976679e1b345307d9c02057f02aa95528f (sinf/cosf, 2018-08-14).
Arm's MIT relicensing at 11253b0b9d6b covers the same algorithms and
constants; current Arm fe09d4e7ed62aa24c81b5791c810bc9de70006e9
still offers MIT. Its source notices read:

    Copyright (c) 2018-2024, Arm Limited.
    Copyright (c) 2018-2019, Arm Limited.

The transcription follows glibc 2.43 sysdeps/ieee754/flt-32/s_sinf.c,
